body:
> This feels more like a feature rhan a strict bugfix

I have not tested it and not sure how C# is calling these interops, but after quickly looking at a code it seems to be a real issue. We had the similar issue with GDNative arguments back in the early 3.x development stages, but it should be relevant for small return value (under 2×pointer size, like Vector2), since these are passed in registers.

If it's working like I think, it's definitely a fix.

Here's a simple test app to demonstrate the issue:
```c++
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstdio>

struct B {
    float a;
    float b;
};

struct A {
    uint8_t a[sizeof(B)];
};

A test() {
    A x;
    B *dst = (B*)&x;
    dst->a = 100.0;
    dst->b = 200.0;
    float z = 300.0 + 400.0; // do some useless math to change XMM registers.
    return x;
}

typedef B (*F)();

int main() {
    {
        F func = (F)(void*)&test;
        B x = func(); // call function using signature "B func()"

        printf("%f %f\n", x.a, x.b); // prints garbage.
    }
    {
        A y = test(); // call function directly.
        B *x = (B *)&y;

        printf("%f %f\n", x->a, x->b); // prints correct values.
    }
}
```
Output (should be reproducible on any x86-64 or ARM64 Linux or macOS system, but not on Windows):
```
32.000000 3216969627526739525632.000000
100.000000 200.00000
```
